{"id":55,"course_id":17,"title":"Text Pipelines for Operators","summary":"Use grep, sort, uniq, jq, and yq-style thinking to extract evidence.","sequence":4,"body_simplified":"## Mental model\nProduction debugging produces too much text. Operators need pipelines that reduce output into counts, suspicious fields, and exact resource names without deleting context.\n\n## What to practice\nPractice filtering events by reason, extracting image tags, counting namespaces, and comparing rendered YAML sections.\n\n## Practice checkpoint\nTurn raw kubectl JSON or logs into a short incident note with commands and evidence.\n\n## Operator habit\nWrite down the symptom, the evidence, the suspected owner, and the safest next command before changing any live object. This turns scattered platform knowledge into a repeatable engineering workflow.","body_traditional":"## Mental model\nProduction debugging produces too much text. Operators need pipelines that reduce output into counts, suspicious fields, and exact resource names without deleting context.\n\n## What to practice\nPractice filtering events by reason, extracting image tags, counting namespaces, and comparing rendered YAML sections.\n\n## Practice checkpoint\nTurn raw kubectl JSON or logs into a short incident note with commands and evidence.\n\n## Operator habit\nWrite down the symptom, the evidence, the suspected owner, and the safest next command before changing any live object. This turns scattered platform knowledge into a repeatable engineering workflow.","pinyin":"Scenario: You are the platform engineer on call for a realistic team environment.\nChecklist:\n- State the user impact in one sentence.\n- Identify the system boundary and owner.\n- Collect read-only evidence first.\n- Propose the smallest reversible change.\n- Capture what should become a dashboard, alert, runbook, or automation.\nCommands:\n$ kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\n$ kubectl describe pod POD_NAME -n NAMESPACE\n$ kubectl logs deploy/APP -n NAMESPACE --since=15m","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-linux-command-line-foundations","course_category":"Linux","course_era":"Platform Academy","vocabulary":[{"id":246,"simplified":"Linux and Command Line Foundations","traditional":"Linux and Command Line Foundations","pinyin":"Linux","definition":"Learn the shell, files, processes, permissions, logs, and package basics every Kubernetes operator needs before touching clusters."},{"id":247,"simplified":"Evidence","traditional":"Evidence","pinyin":"Debugging","definition":"Observed facts from commands, dashboards, logs, traces, events, or cloud APIs."},{"id":248,"simplified":"Blast radius","traditional":"Blast radius","pinyin":"Reliability","definition":"The set of users, tenants, workloads, or systems affected by a failure or change."},{"id":249,"simplified":"Rollback","traditional":"Rollback","pinyin":"Operations","definition":"A planned path to return to a known-good state when a change causes harm."},{"id":250,"simplified":"Runbook","traditional":"Runbook","pinyin":"Operations","definition":"A step-by-step guide that helps responders diagnose and mitigate an incident safely."}],"flashcards":[{"id":148,"lesson_id":55,"prompt":"What is the main goal of Text Pipelines for Operators?","answer":"Use grep, sort, uniq, jq, and yq-style thinking to extract evidence.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":149,"lesson_id":55,"prompt":"Why collect evidence before changing live systems?","answer":"It reduces guessing, protects users, and makes the fix reviewable.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":150,"lesson_id":55,"prompt":"What should a learner produce after each lab?","answer":"A clear diagnosis, a safe action plan, and a reusable note for future incidents.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
